% The opening of the 10me Solo: six bars of piano before the flute enters.
%
% Written out by hand from the original edition, as an example of what ABC
% can carry — two staves under a brace, a third staff above them that is
% there but silent, chords, triplets, ties across a barline, accents, and a
% left hand that splits into two voices for the last two bars.
%
% url: https://ks15.imslp.org/files/imglnks/usimg/d/da/IMSLP293070-PMLP475553-Altes_Fl_10_ed_psc.pdf
%
% gscore - a musical score editor
% (C) Copyright 2026 Sebastien Tricaud. MIT licence; see LICENSE.

X:1
T:10me Solo pour la Flute
T:Op. 36
C:Joseph Henri Altes
%%score 1 {2 3}
M:3/4
L:1/8
Q:1/4=116
K:Eb

% The flute has nothing to play yet, but it has a staff: six bars of rest,
% so the page shows the player where their line is.
V:1 clef=treble name="Flute"
%%MIDI program 73
z6 | z6 | z6 |
z6 | z6 | z6 |

% Right hand. Each bar opens with three chords in the time of two — that is
% what (3:2:3 says — then a chord, an eighth rest, and an accented chord.
V:2 clef=treble name="Piano"
%%MIDI program 0
(3:2:3!ff![cec'] [cec'] [cec'] [ceg] z !emphasis![ceg]2 | (3:2:3[Aca] [Aca] [Aca] [Ace] z !emphasis![Ace]2 |
(3:2:3[Acf] [Acf] [Acf] [Acd] z !emphasis![Acd]2 | (3:2:3[G=Bdg] [GBdg] [GBdg] ([GBdg]4 |
[G=Bdg]6) | [G=Bdg]4 z2 |

% Left hand: octaves walking down, then a pedal G held underneath the
% triplet above it. The & starts that second voice over the same bar.
V:3 clef=bass
%%MIDI program 0
[C,C]4 [C,C]2 | [A,,A,]4 [A,,A,]2 |
[F,,F,]4 [F,,F,]2 | !emphasis![D,,D,]2 !emphasis![=B,,,=B,,]2 !emphasis![G,,,G,,]2 |
(3:2:3!emphasis!G, G, G, G,2 G,2 & G,,6- | G,2 G,2 G,2 & G,,6 |
